# x - a with IEEE-like results: sub_2_1, then the fix-up. a is guarded (rule R3)
# before the fix-up subtracts it; the core is called with a and guards it itself.
# The fix-up is that of milestone1.md, "IEEE-like results", with r0 = times_inf(t) (exact, so not
# a fused operation) for prod(t, const inf): |z0| is named az here, since a is the operand.
kernel:        sub_2_1_full
input:         x = x0 x1
input:         a
output:        w = w0 w1
value:         x-a
requires:      canonical x
ensures:       extends w core=sub_2_1 lead=sub finite_when=abs_le(x,-2);abs_le(a,-2)
min_precision: 3
source:        sub_2_1, then the fix-up of IEEE-like results (Milestone 1 listing)
license:       core: as sub_2_1; fix-up: MIT
status:        proved
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   proved
ieee_theorem:  proofs/theories/IeeeFull.v sub_2_1_full_correct
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the fix-up selects, after an explicit test that z0 is finite (f), between the core's result and the canonical form of t; F1 to F5 of milestone1.md, "IEEE-like results", state what comes out

g        = guard(a)
(z0, z1) = sub_2_1(x0, x1, a)
t        = sub(x0, g)
az       = abs(z0)
f        = lt(az, const inf)
r0       = times_inf(t)
n        = eq(r0, r0)
r1       = select(n, 0, r0)
w0       = select(f, z0, r0)
w1       = select(f, z1, r1)
